RSpade .env - credentials and secrets
=====================================

This file explains every CREDENTIAL in .env: what it is, how to obtain one, and
what happens when it is not set. It is not a list of every setting - most .env
keys are ordinary configuration and are commented where they appear.

.env is NOT in version control and must never be committed. `.env.dist` is the
template: copy it to `.env` and fill in what you need.

    cp .env.dist .env

Two conventions used throughout:

    REQUIRED    the application will not run, or a feature will refuse to work,
                until you set it
    OPTIONAL    unset means the feature is simply off - nothing breaks

RSpade ships NO default passwords or API keys. Every secret below is blank in
the distributed template on purpose: a framework with a built-in password is a
framework where every install in the world shares one.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REQUIRED TO RUN
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

APP_KEY                                                              REQUIRED
    The application encryption key. Laravel uses it for encrypted cookies and
    anything passed through the Crypt facade; losing or changing it invalidates
    that data.

    Generate one:

        php artisan key:generate

    Never share it, never reuse one between environments, and never commit it.


APP_URL                                                              REQUIRED
    The single source of the application hostname. Every generated URL, the
    realtime WebSocket endpoint, and mail links derive from it.

        APP_URL=https://app.example.com     a real host with TLS in front
        APP_URL=https://$HOSTNAME           resolves to the OS hostname at boot
        APP_URL=http://localhost:8080       development only (see below)

    The literal token $HOSTNAME - unquoted, no braces, which is the only
    spelling that survives the .env parser - is replaced with the machine's own
    hostname at boot, so every box can ship the same line.

    Outside development mode APP_URL MUST be https, and the application throws
    at boot if it is not: debug and production emit a Secure session cookie,
    which a browser discards on a plain-http page, leaving every request
    silently unauthenticated. Development also accepts http, so a local
    container with no TLS in front of it works as-is.

    Include the port whenever it is not the scheme default. A container
    published on 8080 needs http://localhost:8080, or URLs built for the
    browser - the realtime socket in particular - point at the wrong port.


RSPADE_DEFAULT_EMAIL                                                 REQUIRED
RSPADE_DEFAULT_PASSWORD                                              REQUIRED
    The credentials of the FIRST user, created by the framework migration when
    you first run `php artisan migrate`. This is the account you log in with.

        RSPADE_DEFAULT_EMAIL=you@example.com
        RSPADE_DEFAULT_PASSWORD=<choose a password>

    Both are blank in the template and RSpade supplies no default. The
    migration REFUSES to create the user while either is blank and tells you
    so - a deliberate stop, so that a new project chooses its own first login
    instead of inheriting one published in the framework's own source.

    Set them BEFORE the first `migrate`. If you have already migrated and want
    to change the password afterwards, change it in the application rather than
    here - the migration only reads these once, when it creates the account.

    These are also the credentials the login form will pre-fill for you, but
    only when RSPADE_LOGIN_AUTOFILL is on - see the next entry.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DEVELOPER CONVENIENCES (careful - these open doors)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

RSPADE_LOGIN_AUTOFILL                                                OPTIONAL
    Whether the login form arrives with RSPADE_DEFAULT_EMAIL and
    RSPADE_DEFAULT_PASSWORD already filled in, so you are not retyping
    credentials all day.

        RSPADE_LOGIN_AUTOFILL=true

    On or off. Nothing else decides it - not the hostname, not the RSX mode.
    The first-run setup screen offers a checkbox and writes this key when you
    tick it; clearing the value turns it back off.

    BLANK IS THE DEFAULT AND THE SAFE SETTING. Auto-fill needs BOTH this key
    and non-blank credentials; either one alone fills in nothing.

    Understand what it does before switching it on: it puts a working login on
    a page that has not authenticated anybody, and it means your password is
    sitting in .env in plain text. Turn it on for a local development install
    and leave it off anywhere another person can reach the address. .env
    travels when you copy it to another machine - and so does this.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DATABASE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DB_HOST, DB_PORT, DB_DATABASE                                        REQUIRED
DB_USERNAME, DB_PASSWORD                                             REQUIRED
    Connection details for the application's MySQL database. In the RSpade
    development container these are provisioned for you and already correct.

    On your own server, create the database and a user scoped to it:

        CREATE DATABASE myapp CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;
        CREATE USER 'myapp'@'localhost' IDENTIFIED BY '<password>';
        GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON myapp.* TO 'myapp'@'localhost';

    Give the application its own user with rights to its own database. Do not
    point it at the MySQL root account.

DB_TEST_DATABASE                                                     OPTIONAL
    The database the test suite runs against; defaults to rspade_test. It must
    be a DIFFERENT database from DB_DATABASE - the test runner refuses to run
    when they are the same, because tests destroy data.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
EMAIL DELIVERY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

MAIL_MAILER, MAIL_HOST, MAIL_PORT, MAIL_ENCRYPTION                   OPTIONAL
MAIL_USERNAME, MAIL_PASSWORD                                         OPTIONAL
    SMTP credentials from your mail provider. Leave the defaults for local
    development, where mail is not delivered out.

    MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS and MAIL_FROM_NAME are what recipients see; set them to
    an address on a domain you control, or providers will reject the mail.

MAILGUN_SECRET, MAILGUN_DOMAIN, MAILGUN_ENDPOINT                     OPTIONAL
POSTMARK_TOKEN                                                       OPTIONAL
    API credentials for those providers, used instead of SMTP when
    MAIL_MAILER names them. Obtain from the provider's dashboard; both are
    account secrets with full sending rights.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SMS DELIVERY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SMS_PROVIDER                                                         OPTIONAL
    Which SMS service to use. The matching credentials below must be set.

SMS_TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID, SMS_TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN                        OPTIONAL
    From the Twilio console. The auth token can send messages and spend money
    on your account - treat it as a password.

SMS_VONAGE_API_KEY, SMS_VONAGE_API_SECRET                            OPTIONAL
SMS_AWS_KEY, SMS_AWS_SECRET, SMS_AWS_REGION                          OPTIONAL
    Equivalents for Vonage and AWS SNS.

SMS_FROM_NUMBER                                                      OPTIONAL
    The number messages come from; provisioned through your provider.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HUMAN VERIFICATION (Cloudflare Turnstile)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

TURNSTILE_ENABLED                                                    OPTIONAL
TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY, TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY                             OPTIONAL
    Bot protection for login, registration and password-reset forms.

    Create a widget at https://dash.cloudflare.com (Turnstile) and copy both
    values. The SITE key is public and appears in page markup; the SECRET key
    is server-side only and must never reach the browser.

    Cloudflare publishes always-pass and always-fail testing keys, which are
    the right choice for local development. With the feature disabled the form
    field is still submitted, carrying a sentinel value - that is by design.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SINGLE SIGN-ON
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SSO_ENABLED                                                          OPTIONAL
SSO_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID, SSO_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET                       OPTIONAL
SSO_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID, SSO_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET                 OPTIONAL
SSO_MICROSOFT_TENANT                                                 OPTIONAL
SSO_FACEBOOK_CLIENT_ID, SSO_FACEBOOK_CLIENT_SECRET                   OPTIONAL
    OAuth application credentials from each provider's developer console. In
    every case you register an application, declare a redirect URI on your own
    domain, and receive an ID (public) and a secret (never public).

    The redirect URI must match APP_URL exactly, scheme and port included, or
    the provider rejects the sign-in.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MAPS, GEOCODING AND IP LOCATION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY                                                  OPTIONAL
HERE_API_KEY, MAPBOX_API_KEY, LOCATIONIQ_API_KEY, OPENCAGE_API_KEY   OPTIONAL
    Address geocoding and map rendering. Set the key for whichever service the
    geocoding settings name. Restrict the key to your domains in the
    provider's console - an unrestricted maps key is billable by anyone who
    finds it, and it appears in page markup.

MAXMIND_ACCOUNT_ID, MAXMIND_LICENSE_KEY                              OPTIONAL
IP2LOCATION_API_KEY, IPINFO_API_KEY, IPSTACK_API_KEY                 OPTIONAL
    IP-to-location lookups. Server-side only; never exposed to the browser.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ANALYTICS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

GOOGLE_ANALYTICS_ID                                                  OPTIONAL
    A measurement ID (G-XXXXXXXXXX). Public by nature - it ships in page
    markup. Loading it also requires the analytics script to be declared as an
    external resource, so that the Content-Security-Policy admits it.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
OBJECT STORAGE AND CACHING
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID, AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY                             OPTIONAL
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION, AWS_BUCKET, AWS_URL, AWS_ENDPOINT                OPTIONAL
    S3-compatible storage credentials, used when FILESYSTEM_DISK is s3. Create
    an IAM user scoped to the one bucket - not an account-wide key.

REDIS_HOST, REDIS_PORT, REDIS_PASSWORD                               OPTIONAL
    Redis connection. RSpade uses Redis for caching, realtime message
    delivery, and short-lived counters. In the development container it is
    local and unauthenticated. A Redis reachable from anywhere but localhost
    MUST have a password set.

MEMCACHED_USERNAME, MEMCACHED_PASSWORD                               OPTIONAL
    Only when using memcached as the cache driver.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
INTERNAL SERVICES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LOCK_SERVER_HOST, LOCK_SERVER_PORT                                   REQUIRED
    The rsx-lockd cluster lock daemon. Not a credential, but load-bearing:
    application locks are granted here, and code that takes a lock against an
    address nothing is listening on waits with no error. The development
    container runs it locally and these are already correct.

REALTIME_ENABLED, REALTIME_WS_PORT                                   OPTIONAL
    The realtime WebSocket relay. The browser reaches it through the web
    server at /ws, so this port is internal only and need not be published.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DEVELOPMENT-ONLY FLAGS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

RSX_MODE                                                             REQUIRED
    development, debug or production. Do not hand-edit this: switch with

        php artisan rsx:mode:set dev|debug|prod

    This is the ONLY mode switch. Laravel's application environment and its
    debug flag are derived from it, so there is no APP_ENV or APP_DEBUG to
    set: production means production, with detailed error pages off.

CONSOLE_DEBUG_ENABLED                                                OPTIONAL
    Channel-filtered debug output in both PHP and JavaScript. Stripped
    entirely from production builds.

IS_FRAMEWORK_DEVELOPER                                               OPTIONAL
    Framework development only. Leave unset in an application; setting it
    changes where generated migrations land and enables framework-only tooling.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
IF YOU LEAK A SECRET
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Rotate it at the source first - the provider's console - then update .env and
restart. Changing .env alone does not invalidate a key that is already loose.

For APP_KEY specifically, rotating invalidates every encrypted value and every
signed cookie: users are logged out, and anything stored with Crypt becomes
unreadable. Plan that one.

Check whether the secret reached version control:

    git log -p --all -- .env

.env is ignored by default, but a copy saved under another name may not be.
